<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Generalization on BFCL-v3.</span>
To examine generalization, we evaluate the <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AgentRL</span> model (trained on ALFWorld, DB, KG, OS, and Webshop) on the BFCL-v3 benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Patil et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
As shown in Table <a href="#S4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 4.1 Main Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ AgentRL: Scaling Agentic Reinforcement Learning with a Multi-Turn, Multi-Task Framework" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AgentRL</span> demonstrates clear improvements on multi-turn tasks and modest gains on single-turn tasks. These results suggest that our approach can enhance the generalizability of function calling, providing a step toward more broadly capable agentic LLMs.</p>
</div>
